<Grid Grid.Row="1"><Grid.ColumnDefinitions><ColumnDefinition Width="780"/><ColumnDefinition Width="*"/></Grid.ColumnDefinitions><Viewbox Stretch="Uniform"><Canvas Width="664" Height="220" ClipToBounds="True"><Image x:Name="OriginalPhoto" Source="{Binding Assets[cronica_source]}" Width="855" Height="1200" Canvas.Left="-28" Canvas.Top="-52"/></Canvas></Viewbox><Grid Grid.Column="1" Margin="10,0,5,10"><Border Height="1" Background="#24221E" VerticalAlignment="Top"/><TextBlock x:Name="PhotoCaption" Text="Buenos Aires (DYN).— The following dispatch was sent from this city by a veteran American war correspondent, who asked not to be identified, who witnessed the Japanese bombing of Pearl Harbor. The cruiser General Belgrano, then Phoenix, emerged unscathed from that attack and became an American symbol of naval resistance." FontSize="9.4" FontFamily="Arial" FontWeight="Bold" TextWrapping="Wrap" TextAlignment="Justify" LineHeight="12" VerticalAlignment="Bottom"/></Grid></Grid>
<Border Grid.Row="1" Grid.RowSpan="3" BorderBrush="#24221E" BorderThickness="1,0,1,1.5"/>
<Viewbox Grid.Row="2" Stretch="Uniform" StretchDirection="DownOnly" Margin="18,0,4,2"><TextBlock x:Name="Headline" Text="“Farewell to a good ship”" FontSize="100" FontFamily="Times New Roman" FontWeight="Bold" FontStyle="Italic"/></Viewbox>
<Grid Grid.Row="3" Margin="14,3,12,16"><Grid.ColumnDefinitions><ColumnDefinition Width="*"/><ColumnDefinition Width="20"/><ColumnDefinition Width="*"/><ColumnDefinition Width="20"/><ColumnDefinition Width="*"/></Grid.ColumnDefinitions><StackPanel Grid.Column="0" x:Name="ArticleColumn1"><TextBlock x:Name="Body0_0" Text="   “I am on the second floor of the Sheraton Hotel in Buenos Aires. From here one overlooks the river and the city, which goes calmly about its business despite the war. Here on the second floor is a press centre where correspondents from all over the world gather. We came to Argentina at the request of our editors, eager for information about the conflict between Argentina and Great Britain. Allow me, for once, a personal digression. As readers back East know, I am an old, grey-haired journalist who witnessed Pearl Harbor.”" FontSize="15" FontFamily="Times New Roman" TextWrapping="Wrap" TextAlignment="Justify" LineHeight="16" LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight"/><TextBlock x:Name="Body0_1" Text="   “I shall never forget that damned morning. Nor the ferocity with which the enemy attacked us, nor the profound sorrow felt by all of us Americans there as we watched the cream of our fleet die such an ugly death.”" FontSize="15" FontFamily="Times New Roman" TextWrapping="Wrap" TextAlignment="Justify" LineHeight="16" LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight"/><TextBlock x:Name="Body0_2" Text="   “But neither shall I forget that one ship proudly survived the Japanese bombs and machine guns. A ship remembered by all the veterans of those days and by the journalists who chronicled those hard times. Her name was Phoenix. She was anchored at our naval base and emerged unscathed from the disaster. Then, and forever afterwards, she became an American symbol of resistance to an unjust, merciless attack by an enemy then both treacherous and superior. We affectionately called her ‘Lucky Phoenix’. I wrote her biography" FontSize="15" FontFamily="Times New Roman" TextWrapping="Wrap" TextAlignment="Justify" LineHeight="16" LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight"/></StackPanel><StackPanel Grid.Column="2" x:Name="ArticleColumn2"><TextBlock x:Name="Body1_0" Text="   for my newspaper. She was a Brooklyn-class light cruiser, built by the New York Shipbuilding Corporation of Camden, New Jersey, and launched on 13 March 1938.”" FontSize="15" FontFamily="Times New Roman" TextWrapping="Wrap" TextAlignment="Justify" LineHeight="16" LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight"/><TextBlock x:Name="Body1_1" Text="   “Scarred and laden with glory, she survived the Second World War. We said goodbye with some sadness. This mistress of the seas was to move from the Pacific to the Atlantic. The Argentines acquired her in 1951.”" FontSize="15" FontFamily="Times New Roman" TextWrapping="Wrap" TextAlignment="Justify" LineHeight="16" LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight"/><TextBlock x:Name="Body1_2" Text="   “In the Argentine Navy she was named General Belgrano, honouring the creator of Argentina’s flag and one of the noblest figures of its War of Independence, comparable to our Francis Marion, Nathanael Greene and Andrew Pickens.”" FontSize="15" FontFamily="Times New Roman" TextWrapping="Wrap" TextAlignment="Justify" LineHeight="16" LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight"/><TextBlock x:Name="Body1_3" Text="   “Last year marked the fortieth anniversary of Pearl Harbor. Surviving veterans and many American organisations asked Argentina to return the illustrious ship. We thought she should come home and, in her final berth, become an iron symbol of our determination to defend freedom.”" FontSize="15" FontFamily="Times New Roman" TextWrapping="Wrap" TextAlignment="Justify" LineHeight="16" LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight"/><TextBlock x:Name="Body1_4" Text="   “The Argentines understood our request but told us the ship was still doing her job. She was not modern, nor equipped, for example, for anti-submarine warfare. But her work was peaceful. She was the best sentinel the South Americans had in the south, guarding their extensive coastline and the harsh seas on the very edge of Antarctica. We insisted that a ship has the right to remain at" FontSize="15" FontFamily="Times New Roman" TextWrapping="Wrap" TextAlignment="Justify" LineHeight="16" LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight"/></StackPanel><StackPanel Grid.Column="4" x:Name="ArticleColumn3"><TextBlock x:Name="Body2_0" Text="   sea until the end and then enter her rightful rest, honoured by those who know her glory and those learning to love her.”" FontSize="15" FontFamily="Times New Roman" TextWrapping="Wrap" TextAlignment="Justify" LineHeight="16" LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight"/><TextBlock x:Name="Body2_1" Text="   “Sadly today—Monday—has been a day of mourning, not only for Argentines but also for those of us who knew, loved and admired her. General Belgrano, the undefeated Phoenix which escaped enemy machine guns and bombs, has fallen to modern technology. A British nuclear submarine destroyed her in a grossly unequal fight.”" FontSize="15" FontFamily="Times New Roman" TextWrapping="Wrap" TextAlignment="Justify" LineHeight="16" LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight"/><TextBlock x:Name="Body2_2" Text="   “Phoenix, General Belgrano, now lies on the floor of the Atlantic.”" FontSize="15" FontFamily="Times New Roman" TextWrapping="Wrap" TextAlignment="Justify" LineHeight="16" LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight"/><TextBlock x:Name="Body2_3" Text="   “Here at the Sheraton, even an old veteran like me feels like crying. It is an unjust end. She was virtually defenceless against the full force of the most advanced submarine technology. More painful still for an American, she was betrayed by satellite intelligence.”" FontSize="15" FontFamily="Times New Roman" TextWrapping="Wrap" TextAlignment="Justify" LineHeight="16" LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight"/><TextBlock x:Name="Body2_4" Text="   “The Argentines accepted the sinking and news of the many victims with quiet dignity. We who knew the ship and saw her fight at Pearl Harbor know she went down as what she was: more than a memory of brave men, a living embodiment of heroes of the sea.”" FontSize="15" FontFamily="Times New Roman" TextWrapping="Wrap" TextAlignment="Justify" LineHeight="16" LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight"/><Border Height="10"/><TextBlock x:Name="Body2_6" Text="   “And as we note how it happened, we Americans, who until now had been involved only in a parallel, interminable mediation, suddenly realise that we too are involved in the conflict. With Phoenix, something of ourselves has also gone down.”" FontSize="15" FontFamily="Times New Roman" TextWrapping="Wrap" TextAlignment="Justify" LineHeight="16" LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight"/></StackPanel></Grid>
